problems.cpp_vs_py:
A trip with k intermediate stops uses k+1 flight legs. Bellman- Ford's i-th round computes optimal prices for paths of AT MOST i edges, because each round extends every previously known path by exactly one more edge. The SNAPSHOT copy is the subtle part: if we relaxed in-place, one round could chain two edges (u->v then v->w) and silently count two legs as one — exceeding the budget. So: k stops ⇔ k+1 edges ⇔ k+1 rounds. For the same reason, plain Dijkstra fails here: it settles each node once with the cheapest price, but that price may spend the whole hop budget, blocking a slightly pricier but fewer-hop route to the destination. ===================================================================== /

